<td id="S4.T3.2.19.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">1692.0</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.19.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">.002</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.19.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">.006</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.19.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">0.305</td>
<td id="S4.T3.2.19.7" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">**</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<p id="S4.T3.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span id="S4.T3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Note.<span id="S4.T3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Pairwise comparisons for variables with significant Kruskal–Wallis results. <math id="S4.T3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathrm{adj}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>adj</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathrm{adj}}</annotation></semantics></math> values are Bonferroni-corrected. Significance codes: <math id="S4.T3.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{*}p_{\mathrm{adj}}&lt;.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>∗</mo></msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>adj</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{*}p_{\mathrm{adj}}&lt;.05</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{}^{**}p_{\mathrm{adj}}&lt;.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mmultiscripts><mi>p</mi><mprescripts></mprescripts><mi>adj</mi><mrow></mrow><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></mmultiscripts><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{**}p_{\mathrm{adj}}&lt;.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.T3.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{}^{***}p_{\mathrm{adj}}&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∗</mo><mo lspace="0em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mi>p</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>adj</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{}^{***}p_{\mathrm{adj}}&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S4.T3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> = Mann–Whitney statistic; <math id="S4.T3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> = rank-biserial effect size.</span></span></p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="124.69" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 124.69" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,124.69) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.49 L 0 120.2 C 0 122.68 2.01 124.69 4.49 124.69 L 472.89 124.69 C 475.37 124.69 477.38 122.68 477.38 120.2 L 477.38 4.49 C 477.38 2.01 475.37 0 472.89 0 L 4.49 0 C 2.01 0 0 2.01 0 4.49 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.55 4.49 L 0.55 120.2 C 0.55 122.37 2.32 124.14 4.49 124.14 L 472.89 124.14 C 475.06 124.14 476.82 122.37 476.82 120.2 L 476.82 4.49 C 476.82 2.32 475.06 0.55 472.89 0.55 L 4.49 0.55 C 2.32 0.55 0.55 2.32 0.55 4.49 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.43 9.15)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.28em;--ltx-fo-height:7.88em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="111.77" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 109.08)" width="460.5"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS1.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.28em;">
<span id="S4.SS1.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Summary.</span><span id="S4.SS1.p4.pic1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Medium CAs consistently produced significantly higher perceived Intelligence, Enjoyment, Anthropomorphism, Intention to Adopt, Trust, and Likeability than Low CAs, and also yielded significantly greater perceived Intelligence and Likeability than High CAs. This pattern suggests an inverted-U relationship between personality expression level and user experience.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2. </span>Personality Alignment and User Perceptions (RQ2)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Personality alignment scores between users and CAs (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.52" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.52</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.52</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SD=0.21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SD=0.21</annotation></semantics></math>, range <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=0</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.88" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.88</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.88</annotation></semantics></math>) showed consistent positive associations between personality alignment and user perceptions. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.2. Personality Alignment and User Perceptions (RQ2) ‣ 4. Results ‣ Vibe Check: Understanding the Effects of LLM-Based Conversational Agents’ Personality and Alignment on User Perceptions in Goal-Oriented Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, the distribution of alignment scores exhibits substantial within-condition variability that enabled examining alignment effects beyond experimental manipulations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09870v1/alignment_score_distributions.svg" id="S4.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/294;" width="476" height="294"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8. </span>Distribution of user–CA Personality Alignment Scores (0–1) by experimental condition (<math id="S4.F8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=50</annotation></semantics></math> per group). Dots show participants; boxplots show medians and interquartile ranges. The medium condition exhibits the highest and tightest alignment (median <math id="S4.F8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx.72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>.72</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx.72</annotation></semantics></math>), the high condition shows a wider midrange spread (median <math id="S4.F8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx.54" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>.54</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx.54</annotation></semantics></math>), and the low condition has the lowest alignment (median <math id="S4.F8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx.30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≈</mo><mn>.30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx.30</annotation></semantics></math>).</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4. </span>Spearman correlations with personality alignment score; *** <math id="S4.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq.001</annotation></semantics></math>, ** <math id="S4.T4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq.01</annotation></semantics></math>, * <math id="S4.T4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\leq.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\leq.05</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T4.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T4.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Variable</th>
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><math id="S4.T4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<th id="S4.T4.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Sig.</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T4.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Intelligence</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_t">.253</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">**</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Enjoyment</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">.234</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">**</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Anthropomorphism</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">.231</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">**</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Intention to Adopt</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">.269</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">***</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Trust</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right">.288</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">***</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">Likeability</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_border_bb">.304</td>
<td id="S4.T4.2.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">***</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Spearman’s rank correlations indicated positive relationships between personality alignment and all variables, as shown in Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2. Personality Alignment and User Perceptions (RQ2) ‣ 4. Results ‣ Vibe Check: Understanding the Effects of LLM-Based Conversational Agents’ Personality and Alignment on User Perceptions in Goal-Oriented Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Likeability (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=.304" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>.304</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=.304</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>) was strongest, followed by Trust (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=.288" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>.288</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=.288</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>), Intention to Adopt (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=.269" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>.269</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=.269</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>), Intelligence (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=.253" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>.253</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=.253</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.002" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.002</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.002</annotation></semantics></math>), Enjoyment (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=.234" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>.234</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=.234</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.004" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.004</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.004</annotation></semantics></math>), and Anthropomorphism (<math id="S4.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=.231" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>.231</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=.231</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.005</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">While personality alignment showed significant bivariate correlations with user perceptions, hierarchical regression analyses controlling for experimental condition revealed more limited effects. Trust showed a significant incremental contribution from personality alignment (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.397" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.397</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.397</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.084" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.084</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.084</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta R^{2}=.025" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>.025</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta R^{2}=.025</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.049" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.049</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.049</annotation></semantics></math>), and Intelligence demonstrated a similar pattern (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.185" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.185</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.185</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.076" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.076</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.076</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta R^{2}=.018" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>.018</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta R^{2}=.018</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.094" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.094</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.094</annotation></semantics></math>). Enjoyment (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.347" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.347</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.347</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.196" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.196</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.196</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta R^{2}=.012" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>.012</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta R^{2}=.012</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.172" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.172</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.172</annotation></semantics></math>), Intention to Adopt (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=1.728" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.728</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=1.728</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.156" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.156</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.156</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta R^{2}=.016" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>.016</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta R^{2}=.016</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.116" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.116</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.116</annotation></semantics></math>), and Likeability (<math id="S4.SS2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=0.790" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.790</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=0.790</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.188" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.188</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.188</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta R^{2}=.009" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>.009</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta R^{2}=.009</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS2.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.234" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.234</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.234</annotation></semantics></math>) showed consistent directional trends in the expected direction. These effect sizes are practically meaningful, with alignment improvements translating to substantial changes in user perceptions. However, the current sample size constrains statistical power. These findings underscore the importance of personality alignment but suggest that larger samples are necessary to reliably detect incremental effects.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="91.48" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 91.48" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,91.48) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.49 L 0 86.99 C 0 89.47 2.01 91.48 4.49 91.48 L 472.89 91.48 C 475.37 91.48 477.38 89.47 477.38 86.99 L 477.38 4.49 C 477.38 2.01 475.37 0 472.89 0 L 4.49 0 C 2.01 0 0 2.01 0 4.49 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.55 4.49 L 0.55 86.99 C 0.55 89.17 2.32 90.93 4.49 90.93 L 472.89 90.93 C 475.06 90.93 476.82 89.17 476.82 86.99 L 476.82 4.49 C 476.82 2.32 475.06 0.55 472.89 0.55 L 4.49 0.55 C 2.32 0.55 0.55 2.32 0.55 4.49 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.43 9.15)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.28em;--ltx-fo-height:5.48em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="78.56" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 75.87)" width="460.5"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.28em;">
<span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Summary.</span><span id="S4.SS2.p4.pic1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Personality alignment demonstrated consistent positive correlations with all six variables, suggesting that beyond the main effects of CA personality design, individual user–agent personality alignment contributes meaningfully to user experience.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3. </span>Individual Trait Distance Effects (RQ3)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Analysis of individual trait distances revealed differential impacts on user perceptions. Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.3. Individual Trait Distance Effects (RQ3) ‣ 4. Results ‣ Vibe Check: Understanding the Effects of LLM-Based Conversational Agents’ Personality and Alignment on User Perceptions in Goal-Oriented Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports Spearman correlations between each trait mismatch and the variables. Conscientiousness distance showed the strongest and most pervasive negative associations, correlating with Intelligence (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.231" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.231</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.231</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.005</annotation></semantics></math>) and Likeability (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.288" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.288</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.288</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>); its association with Trust was non-significant (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.146" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.146</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.146</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.075" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.075</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.075</annotation></semantics></math>). Extraversion distance was broadly impactful, relating negatively to Intelligence (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.232" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.232</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.232</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.004" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.004</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.004</annotation></semantics></math>), Intention to Adopt (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.227" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.227</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.227</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.005" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.005</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.005</annotation></semantics></math>), Trust (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.202" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.202</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.202</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.013" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.013</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.013</annotation></semantics></math>), and Likeability (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.271" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.271</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.271</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.001</annotation></semantics></math>). Emotional Stability distance influenced evaluative judgments like Intelligence (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.222" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.222</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.222</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.006" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.006</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.006</annotation></semantics></math>), Intention to Adopt (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.166" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.166</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.166</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.042" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.042</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.042</annotation></semantics></math>), Trust (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.191" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.191</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.191</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.020" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.020</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.020</annotation></semantics></math>), and Likeability (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.233" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.233</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.233</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.004" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.004</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.004</annotation></semantics></math>). Agreeableness distance impacted Enjoyment (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.208" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.208</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.208</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.011" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.011</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.011</annotation></semantics></math>), Intention to Adopt (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.203" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.203</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.203</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.013" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.013</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.013</annotation></semantics></math>), and Trust (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.188" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.188</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.188</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.021" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.021</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.021</annotation></semantics></math>). Openness distance showed significant associations with two variables: Intention to Adopt (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.164" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.164</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.164</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.045" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.045</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.045</annotation></semantics></math>) and Trust (<math id="S4.SS3.p1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{s}=-.201" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>.201</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{s}=-.201</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.014" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.014</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.014</annotation></semantics></math>), while showing non-significant correlations with the remaining four variables. The consistent negative direction of these correlations indicates that greater mismatches between user and CA personality traits are associated with lower values of the variables.</p>
</div>
