<p id="S4.T5.3" class="ltx_p ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_left"><span id="S4.T5.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Note.</span> * <math id="S4.T5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.05</annotation></semantics></math>, ** <math id="S4.T5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.01</annotation></semantics></math>, *** <math id="S4.T5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>. Trait distance variables reflect the absolute difference between participant and CA scores on each trait. Negative correlations indicate that greater trait distance is associated with lower values of the variables.</p></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="157.9" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 477.38 157.9" width="477.38"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,157.9) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 4.49 L 0 153.41 C 0 155.89 2.01 157.9 4.49 157.9 L 472.89 157.9 C 475.37 157.9 477.38 155.89 477.38 153.41 L 477.38 4.49 C 477.38 2.01 475.37 0 472.89 0 L 4.49 0 C 2.01 0 0 2.01 0 4.49 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0.55 4.49 L 0.55 153.41 C 0.55 155.58 2.32 157.35 4.49 157.35 L 472.89 157.35 C 475.06 157.35 476.82 155.58 476.82 153.41 L 476.82 4.49 C 476.82 2.32 475.06 0.55 472.89 0.55 L 4.49 0.55 C 2.32 0.55 0.55 2.32 0.55 4.49 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 8.43 9.15)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:33.28em;--ltx-fo-height:10.28em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="144.98" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 142.29)" width="460.5"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:33.28em;">
<span id="S4.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Summary.</span><span id="S4.SS3.p2.pic1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"> Individual trait distances showed differential effects. Conscientiousness mismatches most negatively affected Intelligence and Likeability. Extraversion mismatches were broadly impactful, relating to Intelligence, Anthropomorphism, Intention to Adopt, Trust, and Likeability. Emotional Stability mismatches reduced Intelligence, Intention to Adopt, Trust, and Likeability. Agreeableness mismatches lowered Enjoyment, Intention to Adopt, and Trust. Openness mismatches had comparatively minimal impact, with small effects on Intention to Adopt and Trust.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4. </span>Personality Alignment Clusters (RQ4)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">k-means clustering based on trait mismatch patterns identified three distinct user groups, jointly supported by silhouette analysis (score <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=.310" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>.310</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=.310</annotation></semantics></math>) and elbow-curve (see Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Additional Analyses ‣ Vibe Check: Understanding the Effects of LLM-Based Conversational Agents’ Personality and Alignment on User Perceptions in Goal-Oriented Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a> for details). Figure <a href="#S4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 4.4. Personality Alignment Clusters (RQ4) ‣ 4. Results ‣ Vibe Check: Understanding the Effects of LLM-Based Conversational Agents’ Personality and Alignment on User Perceptions in Goal-Oriented Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> depicts the resulting profiles.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.09870v1/cluster_profiles_radar_chart.svg" id="S4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/335;" width="476" height="335"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9. </span>Personality alignment clusters from <math id="S4.F9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-means on trait distances (Openness, Conscientiousness, Extraversion, Agreeableness, Emotional Stability; 0–1) plus overall Personality Alignment Score. The radar plot shows three profiles: Extraversion-Misaligned (purple) has a large Extraversion distance with otherwise moderate distances and a mid alignment score; Globally-Misaligned (green) shows high distances on all traits and the lowest alignment; Well-Aligned (red) exhibits uniformly small distances and the highest alignment score.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Cluster 0 (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=39" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>39</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=39</annotation></semantics></math>, 26.0%) represented “Extraversion-Misaligned” users, characterized by high extraversion distance mismatches (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=3.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=3.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SD=0.67" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.67</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SD=0.67</annotation></semantics></math>) with moderate alignment on other traits. This cluster consisted primarily of high-condition participants (92.3%) with a moderate overall alignment score (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.498" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.498</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.498</annotation></semantics></math>). Cluster 1 (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=49</annotation></semantics></math>, 32.7%) comprised “Globally-Misaligned” users showing high trait distance across all traits, particularly Openness (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=3.31" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.31</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=3.31</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SD=0.72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.72</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SD=0.72</annotation></semantics></math>) and Agreeableness (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=3.21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.21</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=3.21</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="SD=0.64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">SD=0.64</annotation></semantics></math>). This cluster contained almost exclusively low-condition participants (98.0%) with the lowest personality alignment (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.287" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.287</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.287</annotation></semantics></math>). Cluster 2 (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=62" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>62</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=62</annotation></semantics></math>, 41.3%) included “Well-Aligned” users with minimal trait distance (all <math id="S4.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&lt;1.14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1.14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&lt;1.14</annotation></semantics></math>) and the highest personality alignment scores (<math id="S4.SS4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.717" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.717</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.717</annotation></semantics></math>). This cluster predominantly featured medium-condition participants (77.4%).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Kruskal–Wallis tests in Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.4. Personality Alignment Clusters (RQ4) ‣ 4. Results ‣ Vibe Check: Understanding the Effects of LLM-Based Conversational Agents’ Personality and Alignment on User Perceptions in Goal-Oriented Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> indicate significant differentiation across all six variables (all <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.05</annotation></semantics></math>; see also Figure <a href="#S4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 4.4. Personality Alignment Clusters (RQ4) ‣ 4. Results ‣ Vibe Check: Understanding the Effects of LLM-Based Conversational Agents’ Personality and Alignment on User Perceptions in Goal-Oriented Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>), suggesting that personality alignment patterns are systematically associated with user perceptions. Post hoc Mann–Whitney <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> comparisons (Table <a href="#S4.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ 4.4. Personality Alignment Clusters (RQ4) ‣ 4. Results ‣ Vibe Check: Understanding the Effects of LLM-Based Conversational Agents’ Personality and Alignment on User Perceptions in Goal-Oriented Tasks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) show that “Well-Aligned” users (Cluster 2) consistently report higher values than the remaining clusters on all significant variables. The largest effects emerge for Enjoyment (Cluster 2 vs. Cluster 1: <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=1062.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1062.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=1062.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=.006" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.006</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=.006</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=.257" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.257</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=.257</annotation></semantics></math>) and Trust (Cluster 2 vs. Cluster 1: <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U=945.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>945.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U=945.5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;.001</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=.323" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>.323</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=.323</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
